= Running the Bonjour Conformance Test against avahi-daemon =

Apple's Bonjour Conformance Test (BCT) checks an mDNS responder
against RFC 6762: probing, tiebreaks, conflicts, announcements, reply
timing, duplicate suppression, service renames and hot-plugging. It is
a macOS command line tool from the Apple developer downloads (an Apple
developer account is needed). The README and the Conformance Test
Outline in the download describe the subtests. This file lists what it
takes to run it against avahi-daemon, because most of the time goes
into the setup, not the test. It was written for BCT 1.5.4.

== Machines ==

Two machines on one Ethernet segment: the Mac that runs BCT and the
device that runs avahi-daemon. Both wired. With the Mac on WiFi the
test does not leave INITIAL PROBING, whatever the device does.

Turn off File Sharing on the Mac for the run. BCT picks the records
for its reply timing subtests from a packet capture that includes the
Mac's own announcements, and with File Sharing on it queries the
device for _smb._tcp, which the device does not have. Check with
"dns-sd -B _smb._tcp local." that nothing on the segment advertises
SMB before you start.

== The device ==

 * Use an IPv4 link-local address (169.254.0.0/16) on the test
   interface. BCT accepts a device only after it sees ARP announcements
   for such an address, and then expects mDNS probes from that address
   within a few seconds. With NetworkManager:

       nmcli con mod <connection> ipv4.method link-local

   Add a route to the Mac's subnet (ipv4.routes) if you need to reach
   the device over the network during the test.

 * The order at "power on" matters. The outline requires the ARP
   announcements before the first mDNS packet. NetworkManager
   announces an address about two seconds after it configures it, and
   avahi-daemon probes as soon as it starts, so a plain reboot or link
   cycle gets the order wrong. Bring the interface up, wait for the
   ARP announcements (or send RFC 5227 announcements yourself), then
   start avahi-daemon.

 * Only one mDNS responder may run on the device. Stop and mask the
   distribution's daemon and its socket for the session:

       systemctl mask --runtime avahi-daemon.service avahi-daemon.socket
       systemctl stop avahi-daemon.service avahi-daemon.socket

   Check that nothing else binds UDP port 5353 (ss -ulpn). Set
   allow-interfaces in avahi-daemon.conf to the test interface, so the
   daemon does not answer on a second interface.

 * BCT tracks every SRV record the device probes for, and it denies
   them to force a rename. D-Bus clients such as Samba register
   services and do not rename on a collision, so set
   disable-user-service-publishing=yes for the run and publish one or
   two static services from the services directory instead. Give them
   literal names: a static service with %h in its name does not rename
   on a service name conflict.

 * Run the daemon in the foreground with --debug and keep the log.
   "Server startup complete", "Deferring probing", "Withdrawing" and
   "Host name conflict" lines line up with the subtests.

== Running BCT ==

BCT needs root and asks yes/no questions, so run it from a root shell
(sudo -s), not through "sudo ... | tee", or the prompt breaks:

    ./BonjourConformanceTest -I <interface> -V -DD -M \
        -Aip <router-ip> -Amac <router-mac> \
        -F results.log 2>&1 | tee console.log

-I is the Mac's interface (en0, en10, see networksetup
-listallhardwareports). -Aip and -Amac exclude the router's own mDNS
traffic. -M enables the manual subtests. Answer yes to the first
question, and when BCT prints "Please Power On Device", do the power
on described above.

Manual subtests: MANUAL NAME CHANGE asks for a service rename. Edit
the <name> in the service file and run "avahi-daemon -r". HOT-PLUGGING
asks for a cable disconnect and reconnect. "nmcli con down" and "up"
on the device passed it in our runs.

== Reading the results ==

The multicast DNS test is the part that applies to avahi-daemon. The
network interoperability and BSP tests need a unicast DNS setup and a
low-power mode and can be skipped. A failed subtest aborts the rest,
so fix them in order. BCT warns that the daemon "probes quickly,
should probe per 250 ms". The probe scheduler batches probes with a
delay of up to 50 ms, so the intervals vary around 250 ms. It is a
warning, not a failure.
